1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,480
In 2003, in a Hampshire village shop,

2
00:00:02,540 --> 00:00:05,019
sub-postmistress Jo Hamilton watched her computer

3
00:00:05,020 --> 00:00:06,785
invent a debt in front of her.

4
00:00:06,805 --> 00:00:08,864
The screen said two thousand pounds was missing

5
00:00:08,865 --> 00:00:10,104
from her post-office till —

6
00:00:10,105 --> 00:00:12,711
money she had never seen.

7
00:00:12,771 --> 00:00:13,850
She phoned the helpline

8
00:00:13,851 --> 00:00:15,530
and followed its instructions —

9
00:00:15,531 --> 00:00:17,431
and while she was on the line,

10
00:00:17,491 --> 00:00:20,209
the missing two thousand became four.

11
00:00:20,269 --> 00:00:22,506
She remortgaged her house to pay it.

12
00:00:22,566 --> 00:00:24,906
Her employer prosecuted her anyway —

13
00:00:24,966 --> 00:00:27,404
for theft, in front of her whole village.

14
00:00:27,464 --> 00:00:29,701
But the money never existed.

15
00:00:29,761 --> 00:00:31,441
The computer was lying.

16
00:00:31,501 --> 00:00:33,266
The people who owned it knew —

17
00:00:33,301 --> 00:00:35,140
and proving that would take twenty-one years,

18
00:00:35,141 --> 00:00:38,953
and an Act of Parliament like no other.

19
00:00:39,013 --> 00:00:40,932
What happened in that village shop happened,

20
00:00:40,933 --> 00:00:44,213
in some form, to roughly a thousand people —

21
00:00:44,273 --> 00:00:47,013
prosecuted on the word of one machine,

22
00:00:47,073 --> 00:00:50,213
most by their own employer, across sixteen years,

23
00:00:50,273 --> 00:00:52,052
while warning after warning was put in writing

24
00:00:52,053 --> 00:00:53,417
and filed away.

25
00:00:53,477 --> 00:00:55,389
England, 1999.

26
00:00:55,449 --> 00:00:58,861
It begins with an upgrade.

27
00:00:58,921 --> 00:01:01,340
A sub-postmaster is not quite a shopkeeper

28
00:01:01,341 --> 00:01:03,619
and not quite a civil servant.

29
00:01:03,679 --> 00:01:04,378
In Britain,

30
00:01:04,379 --> 00:01:06,538
the post office counter usually lives inside

31
00:01:06,539 --> 00:01:07,939
somebody's shop —

32
00:01:07,999 --> 00:01:09,719
the newsagent, the grocer —

33
00:01:09,779 --> 00:01:12,198
and the person behind it is a private citizen

34
00:01:12,199 --> 00:01:14,098
who has bought the right to serve their village

35
00:01:14,099 --> 00:01:17,433
on behalf of a centuries-old national institution.

36
00:01:17,493 --> 00:01:19,869
They hand out pensions on Thursdays.

37
00:01:19,929 --> 00:01:21,980
They take in Christmas savings.

38
00:01:22,040 --> 00:01:23,399
They know which customer needs

39
00:01:23,400 --> 00:01:25,438
the forms read aloud to them.

40
00:01:25,498 --> 00:01:26,877
In thousands of villages,

41
00:01:26,878 --> 00:01:29,257
the sub-postmaster was the closest thing

42
00:01:29,258 --> 00:01:32,138
the community had to a bank, a notary,

43
00:01:32,198 --> 00:01:34,329
and a friend with an official stamp.

44
00:01:34,389 --> 00:01:36,828
The red sign over the door meant the same thing

45
00:01:36,829 --> 00:01:38,908
on every high street in the country:

46
00:01:38,909 --> 00:01:40,888
the state, in walking distance,

47
00:01:40,889 --> 00:01:43,731
with a familiar face behind the counter.

48
00:01:43,791 --> 00:01:45,490
People queued at nine for their pensions

49
00:01:45,491 --> 00:01:47,096
and stayed to talk.

50
00:01:47,156 --> 00:01:49,816
The sub-postmaster cashed their cheques,

51
00:01:49,876 --> 00:01:53,155
weighed their parcels, kept their confidences.

52
00:01:53,215 --> 00:01:54,695
It was a good life —

53
00:01:54,755 --> 00:01:56,892
modest, respected, permanent.

54
00:01:56,952 --> 00:01:59,793
The people in this story did not drift into it.

55
00:01:59,853 --> 00:02:01,252
They bought their way in,

56
00:02:01,253 --> 00:02:03,232
with their savings and their houses,

57
00:02:03,233 --> 00:02:03,872
because running

58
00:02:03,873 --> 00:02:06,252
a village post office was the safest thing

59
00:02:06,253 --> 00:02:09,102
a careful family could do with its money.

60
00:02:09,162 --> 00:02:11,027
People paid for that privilege.

61
00:02:11,087 --> 00:02:13,826
In May 1998, a man named Alan Bates

62
00:02:13,827 --> 00:02:16,686
and his partner Suzanne Sercombe put sixty-five

63
00:02:16,687 --> 00:02:18,486
thousand pounds of their own money

64
00:02:18,487 --> 00:02:20,826
into a small post office in Craig-y-Don,

65
00:02:20,827 --> 00:02:23,030
on the North Wales coast.

66
00:02:23,090 --> 00:02:24,850
He was a careful man —

67
00:02:24,910 --> 00:02:28,010
the kind who kept receipts, filed paperwork,

68
00:02:28,070 --> 00:02:29,739
backed up his backups.

69
00:02:29,799 --> 00:02:31,958
That detail will matter more than anything else

70
00:02:31,959 --> 00:02:33,104
in this film.

71
00:02:33,164 --> 00:02:34,344
In 1999,

72
00:02:34,404 --> 00:02:36,263
the Post Office began rolling out a new computer

73
00:02:36,264 --> 00:02:38,977
system to every branch counter in the country.

74
00:02:39,037 --> 00:02:40,577
It was called Horizon,

75
00:02:40,637 --> 00:02:42,376
built by the British firm ICL

76
00:02:42,377 --> 00:02:44,797
under its Japanese owner, Fujitsu,

77
00:02:44,857 --> 00:02:46,236
and it was described at the time

78
00:02:46,237 --> 00:02:49,680
as the biggest non-military IT contract in Europe.

79
00:02:49,740 --> 00:02:51,059
The terminals arrived the way

80
00:02:51,060 --> 00:02:53,060
the future usually does —

81
00:02:53,120 --> 00:02:54,717
as an instruction.

82
00:02:54,777 --> 00:02:55,696
The paper ledgers

83
00:02:55,697 --> 00:02:57,436
that sub-postmasters had balanced

84
00:02:57,437 --> 00:02:58,917
by hand were carried out,

85
00:02:58,977 --> 00:03:01,716
and from that day the branch's truth was whatever

86
00:03:01,717 --> 00:03:03,516
the screen said it was.

87
00:03:03,576 --> 00:03:05,488
Most branches got on with it.

88
00:03:05,548 --> 00:03:07,028
The machine was modern,

89
00:03:07,088 --> 00:03:08,847
the institution was centuries old,

90
00:03:08,848 --> 00:03:10,867
and nobody buys a village post office expecting

91
00:03:10,868 --> 00:03:12,150
to need a lawyer.

92
00:03:12,210 --> 00:03:14,865
By late 2001, Horizon was everywhere.

93
00:03:14,925 --> 00:03:17,204
And almost at once, in shop after shop,

94
00:03:17,205 --> 00:03:18,364
the same strange scene began

95
00:03:18,365 --> 00:03:20,227
to play out at closing time.

96
00:03:20,287 --> 00:03:22,849
A sub-postmistress cashes up.

97
00:03:22,909 --> 00:03:24,635
She counts the till twice.

98
00:03:24,695 --> 00:03:26,354
The cash in her hand and the number

99
00:03:26,355 --> 00:03:28,315
on the screen do not agree —

100
00:03:28,375 --> 00:03:30,554
the screen says money is missing.

101
00:03:30,614 --> 00:03:31,914
Not a rounding error:

102
00:03:31,974 --> 00:03:33,269
hundreds of pounds.

103
00:03:33,329 --> 00:03:35,769
Sometimes thousands, appearing overnight,

104
00:03:35,829 --> 00:03:38,300
between locking the door and unlocking it.

105
00:03:38,319 --> 00:03:41,779
In Craig-y-Don, Horizon arrived in October 2000;

106
00:03:41,839 --> 00:03:42,598
by December,

107
00:03:42,599 --> 00:03:44,838
Alan Bates was staring at an unexplained hole

108
00:03:44,839 --> 00:03:47,150
of around six thousand pounds.

109
00:03:47,210 --> 00:03:48,890
He did what everyone did.

110
00:03:48,950 --> 00:03:50,490
He rang the helpline.

111
00:03:50,550 --> 00:03:51,049
The search

112
00:03:51,050 --> 00:03:53,089
that came first played out behind thousands

113
00:03:53,090 --> 00:03:54,645
of those counters.

114
00:03:54,705 --> 00:03:56,338
You count the till again.

115
00:03:56,398 --> 00:03:57,938
You count the safe.

116
00:03:57,998 --> 00:03:59,897
You go back through the week's receipts line

117
00:03:59,898 --> 00:04:02,758
by line, hunting for your own mistake —

118
00:04:02,818 --> 00:04:04,700
because it must be your mistake;

119
00:04:04,718 --> 00:04:07,201
the alternative is unthinkable.

120
00:04:07,261 --> 00:04:09,540
Husbands and wives sat up past midnight

121
00:04:09,541 --> 00:04:11,501
with shoeboxes of paper,

122
00:04:11,561 --> 00:04:14,080
checking each other's arithmetic against a machine

123
00:04:14,081 --> 00:04:15,953
that would not show its own.

124
00:04:16,013 --> 00:04:17,786
The screen did not argue.

125
00:04:17,846 --> 00:04:19,758
It simply repeated the number.

126
00:04:19,818 --> 00:04:21,397
The helpline had an answer,

127
00:04:21,398 --> 00:04:23,123
and the answer never changed.

128
00:04:23,183 --> 00:04:25,281
No one else is having this problem.

129
00:04:25,341 --> 00:04:27,578
You are the only one.

130
00:04:27,638 --> 00:04:29,357
The inquiry that finally examined

131
00:04:29,358 --> 00:04:31,777
this scandal heard that sentence described

132
00:04:31,778 --> 00:04:35,338
by sub-postmaster after sub-postmaster —

133
00:04:35,398 --> 00:04:36,597
the same reassurance,

134
00:04:36,598 --> 00:04:38,717
delivered up and down the country,

135
00:04:38,718 --> 00:04:41,118
to hundreds of people at once,

136
00:04:41,178 --> 00:04:43,237
each of them alone in a dark shop

137
00:04:43,238 --> 00:04:45,943
with a number that could not be true.

138
00:04:46,003 --> 00:04:47,729
It was not a small lie.

139
00:04:47,789 --> 00:04:49,979
It was the load-bearing lie.

140
00:04:50,039 --> 00:04:52,438
If you are the only one, the machine is fine —

141
00:04:52,439 --> 00:04:54,598
and the problem is you.

142
00:04:54,658 --> 00:04:55,957
And here the trap closed,

143
00:04:55,958 --> 00:04:58,417
because of a clause most of them had never dwelt

144
00:04:58,418 --> 00:04:59,218
on.

145
00:04:59,230 --> 00:05:00,750
Under their contracts,

146
00:05:00,810 --> 00:05:02,789
sub-postmasters were personally liable

147
00:05:02,790 --> 00:05:04,996
for losses shown in their accounts.

148
00:05:05,056 --> 00:05:06,215
The court that later tore

149
00:05:06,216 --> 00:05:08,676
this system apart put it simply:

150
00:05:08,736 --> 00:05:11,195
Horizon offered no way for a sub-postmaster

151
00:05:11,196 --> 00:05:13,516
to dispute Horizon's own figures.

152
00:05:13,576 --> 00:05:15,635
The screen said the money was gone;

153
00:05:15,656 --> 00:05:18,236
the contract said the shortfall was yours;

154
00:05:18,296 --> 00:05:21,618
and the institution treated an illusion as a debt.

155
00:05:21,678 --> 00:05:23,125
So they paid.

156
00:05:23,185 --> 00:05:24,540
From savings.

157
00:05:24,600 --> 00:05:26,326
From remortgages.

158
00:05:26,386 --> 00:05:27,601
From their parents.

159
00:05:27,661 --> 00:05:28,841
And some of them —

160
00:05:28,901 --> 00:05:31,841
exhausted, frightened, assured they were alone —

161
00:05:31,901 --> 00:05:34,020
pressed the button that said the accounts balanced

162
00:05:34,021 --> 00:05:35,261
when they did not,

163
00:05:35,321 --> 00:05:38,304
just to keep trading until someone sorted it out.

164
00:05:38,364 --> 00:05:41,171
British law has a name for pressing that button.

165
00:05:41,172 --> 00:05:43,012
It is called false accounting,

166
00:05:43,072 --> 00:05:45,127
and it carries a criminal record.

167
00:05:45,187 --> 00:05:47,192
Alan Bates refused to press it.

168
00:05:47,252 --> 00:05:49,871
He refused to sign for phantom losses at all,

169
00:05:49,872 --> 00:05:51,271
and he sent the Post Office letter

170
00:05:51,272 --> 00:05:53,436
after letter saying precisely why.

171
00:05:53,496 --> 00:05:55,056
In November 2003,

172
00:05:55,116 --> 00:05:57,315
the Post Office terminated his contract without

173
00:05:57,316 --> 00:05:58,776
giving a reason —

174
00:05:58,836 --> 00:06:00,895
its own files, disclosed years later,

175
00:06:00,896 --> 00:06:03,024
called him unmanageable.

176
00:06:03,084 --> 00:06:04,083
He lost the shop

177
00:06:04,084 --> 00:06:06,668
and the sixty-five thousand pounds.

178
00:06:06,728 --> 00:06:08,727
What the Post Office did not know was that it

179
00:06:08,728 --> 00:06:10,547
had just fired the one man in Britain

180
00:06:10,548 --> 00:06:14,412
who would never, ever let it go.

181
00:06:14,472 --> 00:06:16,105
Then the letters changed.

182
00:06:16,165 --> 00:06:18,044
Because the Post Office was not only

183
00:06:18,045 --> 00:06:19,565
these people's employer,

184
00:06:19,625 --> 00:06:21,465
their landlord of last resort,

185
00:06:21,525 --> 00:06:23,882
and the author of their contracts.

186
00:06:23,942 --> 00:06:24,821
In England and Wales,

187
00:06:24,822 --> 00:06:26,981
any company may bring a private prosecution —

188
00:06:26,982 --> 00:06:30,869
and the Post Office did, on an industrial scale.

189
00:06:30,929 --> 00:06:32,348
When a shortfall appeared,

190
00:06:32,349 --> 00:06:34,088
the Post Office investigated the crime,

191
00:06:34,089 --> 00:06:36,568
and the Post Office decided the charge,

192
00:06:36,569 --> 00:06:39,760
and the Post Office prosecuted the case.

193
00:06:39,820 --> 00:06:43,032
Victim, detective, and prosecutor, in one body.

194
00:06:43,092 --> 00:06:44,251
The police were not called,

195
00:06:44,252 --> 00:06:46,311
because the police were not needed.

196
00:06:46,318 --> 00:06:49,298
Between 1999 and 2015,

197
00:06:49,358 --> 00:06:51,397
roughly seven hundred sub-postmasters

198
00:06:51,398 --> 00:06:54,398
were prosecuted by the Post Office itself —

199
00:06:54,458 --> 00:06:56,197
on average, about one a week,

200
00:06:56,198 --> 00:06:58,168
for a decade and a half.

201
00:06:58,228 --> 00:07:00,167
And keep one number running from here to the end

202
00:07:00,168 --> 00:07:01,308
of this film:

203
00:07:01,368 --> 00:07:02,447
the number of Post Office

204
00:07:02,448 --> 00:07:04,507
or Fujitsu employees convicted of anything

205
00:07:04,508 --> 00:07:06,688
for what you are about to watch.

206
00:07:06,748 --> 00:07:08,335
It never changes.

207
00:07:08,395 --> 00:07:09,974
The method was consistent enough

208
00:07:09,975 --> 00:07:11,674
that a court would later describe it

209
00:07:11,675 --> 00:07:13,233
in cold detail.

210
00:07:13,293 --> 00:07:15,093
Investigators would arrive,

211
00:07:15,153 --> 00:07:17,873
suspend the sub-postmaster on the spot,

212
00:07:17,933 --> 00:07:19,953
and interview them under caution —

213
00:07:20,013 --> 00:07:21,772
the Post Office's own staff,

214
00:07:21,773 --> 00:07:25,050
playing detective in the shop the suspect owned.

215
00:07:25,110 --> 00:07:27,049
The charge sheet carried two counts:

216
00:07:27,050 --> 00:07:28,879
theft, and false accounting.

217
00:07:28,939 --> 00:07:30,299
Theft was the hammer —

218
00:07:30,359 --> 00:07:32,755
it meant prison, headlines, ruin.

219
00:07:32,815 --> 00:07:34,595
And then, quietly, an offer:

220
00:07:34,655 --> 00:07:36,735
plead guilty to false accounting,

221
00:07:36,795 --> 00:07:38,134
pay back the shortfall,

222
00:07:38,135 --> 00:07:40,207
and the theft charge goes away.

223
00:07:40,267 --> 00:07:42,186
Jo Hamilton learned how the lever worked

224
00:07:42,187 --> 00:07:44,166
when the Post Office said her little shop

225
00:07:44,167 --> 00:07:46,726
in South Warnborough was thirty-six thousand

226
00:07:46,727 --> 00:07:47,937
pounds down.

227
00:07:47,997 --> 00:07:50,376
Its own investigators had reported, in writing,

228
00:07:50,377 --> 00:07:52,835
that there was no evidence of theft.

229
00:07:52,895 --> 00:07:55,355
She was charged with theft anyway —

230
00:07:55,415 --> 00:07:56,875
and offered the trade:

231
00:07:56,935 --> 00:07:58,534
plead guilty to fourteen counts

232
00:07:58,535 --> 00:08:03,034
of false accounting, repay thirty-six thousand,

233
00:08:03,035 --> 00:08:05,054
six hundred and forty-four pounds,

234
00:08:05,055 --> 00:08:06,383
and the theft count disappears.

235
00:08:06,384 --> 00:08:08,763
She took it, the way almost everyone took it,

236
00:08:08,764 --> 00:08:11,003
because the alternative was a trial she

237
00:08:11,004 --> 00:08:12,143
could not afford to lose,

238
00:08:12,144 --> 00:08:15,123
in front of people she had served for years.

239
00:08:15,183 --> 00:08:16,862
A criminal record, for the crime

240
00:08:16,863 --> 00:08:19,231
of believing her employer's computer.

241
00:08:19,291 --> 00:08:20,490
The court's file preserves

242
00:08:20,491 --> 00:08:23,291
the Post Office's condition in its own words:

243
00:08:23,351 --> 00:08:26,110
a plea "on the basis that the loss was due

244
00:08:26,111 --> 00:08:28,351
to the computer not working properly

245
00:08:28,411 --> 00:08:29,980
will not be accepted.

246
00:08:30,040 --> 00:08:32,260
In the case of Noel Thomas —

247
00:08:32,320 --> 00:08:34,559
a Welsh postman of forty-two years' service

248
00:08:34,560 --> 00:08:37,960
who ran the counter at Gaerwen, on Anglesey —

249
00:08:38,020 --> 00:08:40,280
the record is even blunter:

250
00:08:40,340 --> 00:08:42,519
his guilty plea would be accepted only

251
00:08:42,520 --> 00:08:44,319
on the basis that he accepted

252
00:08:44,320 --> 00:08:46,813
that Horizon was working perfectly.

253
00:08:46,873 --> 00:08:48,172
You could have your freedom,

254
00:08:48,173 --> 00:08:50,921
if you agreed the machine was innocent.

255
00:08:50,981 --> 00:08:52,020
Thomas went to prison

256
00:08:52,021 --> 00:08:54,020
for a forty-eight-thousand-pound shortfall

257
00:08:54,021 --> 00:08:55,865
that never existed.

258
00:08:55,925 --> 00:08:57,698
He turned sixty inside.

259
00:08:57,758 --> 00:09:00,137
Around the same era, guidance issued

260
00:09:00,138 --> 00:09:02,577
to Post Office investigators was sorting

261
00:09:02,578 --> 00:09:05,598
the people they interviewed
into racial categories —

262
00:09:05,658 --> 00:09:07,297
a document that would not surface

263
00:09:07,298 --> 00:09:09,236
for another fifteen years.

264
00:09:09,296 --> 00:09:11,904
Not everyone could be pushed to a plea.

265
00:09:11,964 --> 00:09:14,483
Lee Castleton ran the post office in Bridlington,

266
00:09:14,484 --> 00:09:16,430
on the Yorkshire coast.

267
00:09:16,490 --> 00:09:19,689
From January 2004 his screen conjured shortfall

268
00:09:19,690 --> 00:09:21,150
after shortfall —

269
00:09:21,210 --> 00:09:23,549
around twenty-five thousand pounds —

270
00:09:23,550 --> 00:09:26,149
and he refused to accept the debt was real,

271
00:09:26,150 --> 00:09:28,268
because he had counted his own till.

272
00:09:28,307 --> 00:09:30,466
So the Post Office took him to the High Court,

273
00:09:30,467 --> 00:09:31,887
in a civil suit,

274
00:09:31,947 --> 00:09:34,406
where there is no jury and the loser pays

275
00:09:34,407 --> 00:09:35,931
the winner's lawyers.

276
00:09:35,991 --> 00:09:38,631
Castleton had no money for barristers;

277
00:09:38,691 --> 00:09:40,251
he represented himself,

278
00:09:40,311 --> 00:09:43,104
a shopkeeper alone against an institution.

279
00:09:43,164 --> 00:09:44,472
He lost.

280
00:09:44,532 --> 00:09:47,912
And with the verdict came the bill
for the Post Office's costs:

281
00:09:47,972 --> 00:09:50,543
three hundred and twenty-one thousand pounds.

282
00:09:50,544 --> 00:09:51,943
He was bankrupted —

283
00:09:51,944 --> 00:09:55,707
over a phantom debt one-thirteenth that size.

284
00:09:55,767 --> 00:09:57,586
His wife began having seizures brought

285
00:09:57,587 --> 00:09:58,979
on by stress.

286
00:09:59,039 --> 00:10:01,322
His children were bullied at school.

287
00:10:01,382 --> 00:10:03,062
The family lost everything.

288
00:10:03,122 --> 00:10:06,241
The Post Office never collected most of the money.

289
00:10:06,301 --> 00:10:08,120
Collection was not the point.

290
00:10:08,180 --> 00:10:09,279
The point was what happened

291
00:10:09,280 --> 00:10:11,399
to the next sub-postmaster who thought

292
00:10:11,400 --> 00:10:12,692
about fighting.

293
00:10:12,752 --> 00:10:14,710
And then there is Seema Misra.

294
00:10:14,770 --> 00:10:16,749
She ran the post office at West Byfleet,

295
00:10:16,750 --> 00:10:18,029
in Surrey.

296
00:10:18,089 --> 00:10:19,489
In January 2008

297
00:10:19,549 --> 00:10:21,988
an audit found her branch seventy-nine thousand

298
00:10:21,989 --> 00:10:24,088
pounds short on Horizon's ledger.

299
00:10:24,148 --> 00:10:25,868
She had put in her own money;

300
00:10:25,928 --> 00:10:27,727
she had told the helpline again and again

301
00:10:27,728 --> 00:10:30,047
that the system was generating losses she

302
00:10:30,048 --> 00:10:31,586
could not explain.

303
00:10:31,646 --> 00:10:33,466
She was charged with theft —

304
00:10:33,526 --> 00:10:37,245
the sum on the indictment, seventy-four thousand,

305
00:10:37,246 --> 00:10:40,785
six hundred and nine pounds and eighty-four pence,

306
00:10:40,786 --> 00:10:42,325
calculated to the penny by a machine

307
00:10:42,326 --> 00:10:43,648
that could not do arithmetic.

308
00:10:43,649 --> 00:10:46,789
At Guildford Crown Court in October 2010,

309
00:10:46,849 --> 00:10:49,388
the Post Office's expert witness assured the jury

310
00:10:49,389 --> 00:10:51,134
the system was reliable.

311
00:10:51,194 --> 00:10:52,595
She was convicted.

312
00:10:52,655 --> 00:10:55,115
On the eleventh of November 2010 —

313
00:10:55,175 --> 00:10:57,055
her son's tenth birthday —

314
00:10:57,115 --> 00:11:00,056
Seema Misra was sent to prison for fifteen months.

315
00:11:00,107 --> 00:11:01,972
She was eight weeks pregnant.

316
00:11:02,032 --> 00:11:04,031
She served her time at Bronzefield,

317
00:11:04,032 --> 00:11:05,511
and when her second son was born

318
00:11:05,512 --> 00:11:06,912
the following summer,

319
00:11:06,972 --> 00:11:09,238
she was wearing an electronic tag.

320
00:11:09,298 --> 00:11:11,177
A pregnant woman went to prison on the word

321
00:11:11,178 --> 00:11:12,417
of a computer.

322
00:11:12,477 --> 00:11:14,776
That sentence should be impossible to write

323
00:11:14,777 --> 00:11:16,293
about modern Britain.

324
00:11:16,353 --> 00:11:19,053
Now here is the one that is worse:

325
00:11:19,113 --> 00:11:21,613
eleven weeks before her jury came back,

326
00:11:21,673 --> 00:11:23,032
the Post Office had already put

327
00:11:23,033 --> 00:11:24,152
in writing what would happen

328
00:11:24,153 --> 00:11:28,263
if anyone looked too closely at the machine.

329
00:11:28,323 --> 00:11:29,770
August 2010.

330
00:11:29,830 --> 00:11:31,969
An internal report lands on senior desks

331
00:11:31,970 --> 00:11:34,589
at the Post Office, written by the head of product

332
00:11:34,590 --> 00:11:36,061
and branch accounting.

333
00:11:36,121 --> 00:11:37,161
Its subject:

334
00:11:37,221 --> 00:11:38,440
how to respond to challenges

335
00:11:38,441 --> 00:11:40,262
to Horizon's integrity.

336
00:11:40,322 --> 00:11:41,161
Its logic,

337
00:11:41,162 --> 00:11:44,802
in the Post Office's own preserved words, is this:

338
00:11:44,862 --> 00:11:46,241
any independent investigation

339
00:11:46,242 --> 00:11:48,321
of the system would have to be disclosed

340
00:11:48,322 --> 00:11:49,522
in court —

341
00:11:49,582 --> 00:11:50,661
and "any perception

342
00:11:50,662 --> 00:11:53,481
that POL doubts its own systems would mean

343
00:11:53,482 --> 00:11:55,041
that all criminal prosecutions

344
00:11:55,042 --> 00:11:56,955
would have to be stayed.

345
00:11:57,015 --> 00:12:00,434
It would also beg a question for the Court
of Appeal over past prosecutions

346
00:12:00,435 --> 00:12:01,899
and imprisonments."

347
00:12:01,959 --> 00:12:04,753
There it is, in one paragraph, in 2010.

348
00:12:04,813 --> 00:12:05,912
Check the machine honestly,

349
00:12:05,913 --> 00:12:08,350
and every prosecution stops.

350
00:12:08,410 --> 00:12:09,929
Check the machine honestly,

351
00:12:09,930 --> 00:12:12,597
and the ones already in prison get to appeal.

352
00:12:12,657 --> 00:12:14,436
The institution had written down the price

353
00:12:14,437 --> 00:12:15,056
of the truth —

354
00:12:15,057 --> 00:12:18,096
and decided, prosecution by prosecution,

355
00:12:18,097 --> 00:12:19,817
not to pay it.

356
00:12:19,877 --> 00:12:21,796
Seema Misra was convicted eleven weeks

357
00:12:21,797 --> 00:12:23,275
after that report.

358
00:12:23,335 --> 00:12:24,954
The expert who vouched for Horizon

359
00:12:24,955 --> 00:12:27,774
at her trial was a Fujitsu engineer named Gareth

360
00:12:27,775 --> 00:12:28,615
Jenkins,

361
00:12:28,675 --> 00:12:30,654
whose standard assurance was that the system

362
00:12:30,655 --> 00:12:33,654
"will accurately record all data that is submitted

363
00:12:33,655 --> 00:12:36,207
to it and correctly account for it.

364
00:12:36,267 --> 00:12:36,646
The Court

365
00:12:36,647 --> 00:12:38,606
of Appeal would later record he had known

366
00:12:38,607 --> 00:12:40,566
of live Horizon bugs since September

367
00:12:40,567 --> 00:12:42,086
of that same year —

368
00:12:42,087 --> 00:12:44,554
and said nothing about them in his evidence.

369
00:12:44,555 --> 00:12:46,143
Because the bugs were real,

370
00:12:46,155 --> 00:12:48,510
and inside Fujitsu they had names.

371
00:12:48,570 --> 00:12:50,070
The Callendar Square bug,

372
00:12:50,130 --> 00:12:52,012
known about since the mid-2000s,

373
00:12:52,070 --> 00:12:54,104
which duplicated transactions.

374
00:12:54,164 --> 00:12:57,864
The receipts-and-payments mismatch bug of 2010 —

375
00:12:57,924 --> 00:13:00,463
an internal note records discrepancies that vanish

376
00:13:00,464 --> 00:13:02,004
from the counter screen

377
00:13:02,064 --> 00:13:03,423
"but will still show within

378
00:13:03,424 --> 00:13:05,783
the back end branch account,"

379
00:13:05,784 --> 00:13:06,923
quietly corrupting the books

380
00:13:06,924 --> 00:13:08,475
of some forty branches.

381
00:13:08,535 --> 00:13:10,958
Their existence was not folklore.

382
00:13:11,018 --> 00:13:12,778
It was ticketed, logged,

383
00:13:12,838 --> 00:13:14,877
and discussed on the vendor's side of a wall

384
00:13:14,878 --> 00:13:17,388
the sub-postmasters could not see.

385
00:13:17,448 --> 00:13:18,367
And on the far side

386
00:13:18,368 --> 00:13:20,207
of that wall sat something even

387
00:13:20,208 --> 00:13:22,588
the bugs could not match for secrecy:

388
00:13:22,648 --> 00:13:23,527
a support centre

389
00:13:23,528 --> 00:13:26,407
in Bracknell where Fujitsu engineers could reach

390
00:13:26,408 --> 00:13:28,247
into a branch's live accounts and change

391
00:13:28,248 --> 00:13:29,448
the numbers —

392
00:13:29,508 --> 00:13:32,047
without the sub-postmaster ever knowing anyone

393
00:13:32,048 --> 00:13:33,245
had been there.

394
00:13:33,305 --> 00:13:35,564
For years, the Post Office's public position

395
00:13:35,565 --> 00:13:38,375
was that this was flatly impossible.

396
00:13:38,435 --> 00:13:39,515
In 2012,

397
00:13:39,575 --> 00:13:42,595
under pressure from MPs led by James Arbuthnot,

398
00:13:42,655 --> 00:13:44,394
the Post Office appointed independent

399
00:13:44,395 --> 00:13:45,695
investigators —

400
00:13:45,755 --> 00:13:49,395
a small forensic accountancy firm, Second Sight —

401
00:13:49,455 --> 00:13:51,691
and for a moment it looked like daylight.

402
00:13:51,692 --> 00:13:54,031
Their first report, in July 2013,

403
00:13:54,032 --> 00:13:56,971
confirmed two live defects and a support system

404
00:13:56,972 --> 00:13:59,873
that failed the very people it existed to help.

405
00:13:59,933 --> 00:14:02,973
That same month, the Post Office's own barrister,

406
00:14:03,033 --> 00:14:04,173
Simon Clarke,

407
00:14:04,233 --> 00:14:06,873
delivered a devastating legal advice:

408
00:14:06,933 --> 00:14:08,992
the expert evidence underpinning years

409
00:14:08,993 --> 00:14:11,713
of prosecutions was unreliable,

410
00:14:11,773 --> 00:14:14,476
because the expert had known about the bugs.

411
00:14:14,536 --> 00:14:17,516
Weeks later, Clarke recorded something darker.

412
00:14:17,576 --> 00:14:19,395
The Post Office had set up weekly calls

413
00:14:19,396 --> 00:14:21,156
to track Horizon's defects —

414
00:14:21,216 --> 00:14:22,775
and then someone gave an instruction

415
00:14:22,776 --> 00:14:24,875
about the minutes of those calls.

416
00:14:24,935 --> 00:14:27,475
The advice preserves it in one sentence:

417
00:14:27,535 --> 00:14:29,794
the emails and minutes "should be, and have been,

418
00:14:29,795 --> 00:14:31,395
destroyed:

419
00:14:31,455 --> 00:14:33,749
the word 'shredded' was conveyed to me.

420
00:14:33,780 --> 00:14:36,880
The thinking, as recorded, was simpler still:

421
00:14:36,940 --> 00:14:39,918
if it's not minuted, it's not disclosable.

422
00:14:39,978 --> 00:14:41,178
That summer —

423
00:14:41,238 --> 00:14:44,238
the summer its own lawyer used the word shredded —

424
00:14:44,298 --> 00:14:45,837
the Post Office was wearing down

425
00:14:45,838 --> 00:14:47,973
a man named Martin Griffiths.

426
00:14:48,033 --> 00:14:50,232
He had run the post office on Hope Farm Road

427
00:14:50,233 --> 00:14:53,010
in Ellesmere Port since 1995.

428
00:14:53,070 --> 00:14:55,650
From 2009, the shortfalls came,

429
00:14:55,710 --> 00:14:58,710
and the helpline told him what it told everyone —

430
00:14:58,770 --> 00:15:00,129
his family say he was made

431
00:15:00,130 --> 00:15:01,777
to feel he was the only one,

432
00:15:01,790 --> 00:15:04,109
while the same complaint poured in from across

433
00:15:04,110 --> 00:15:05,291
the country.

434
00:15:05,351 --> 00:15:06,911
He repaid and repaid;

435
00:15:06,971 --> 00:15:08,850
his elderly parents put in their life savings,

436
00:15:08,851 --> 00:15:11,257
about sixty-two thousand pounds.

437
00:15:11,317 --> 00:15:13,856
In May 2013 his branch was robbed,

438
00:15:13,857 --> 00:15:16,596
and the Post Office's investigator held him partly

439
00:15:16,597 --> 00:15:18,802
responsible for the stolen money.

440
00:15:18,862 --> 00:15:22,306
In July 2013, it gave him notice of termination.

441
00:15:22,366 --> 00:15:25,326
On the twenty-third of September 2013,

442
00:15:25,386 --> 00:15:27,125
Martin Griffiths deliberately walked in front

443
00:15:27,126 --> 00:15:28,597
of a moving bus.

444
00:15:28,657 --> 00:15:31,265
He died in hospital eighteen days later.

445
00:15:31,325 --> 00:15:32,819
He was fifty-nine.

446
00:15:32,879 --> 00:15:34,338
The Post Office later reached

447
00:15:34,339 --> 00:15:37,020
a confidential settlement with his family.

448
00:15:37,080 --> 00:15:39,039
The public inquiry would eventually be told

449
00:15:39,040 --> 00:15:40,359
of at least thirteen people

450
00:15:40,360 --> 00:15:41,859
who took their own lives in connection

451
00:15:41,860 --> 00:15:44,659
with this scandal, and of at least fifty-nine more

452
00:15:44,660 --> 00:15:46,283
who contemplated it.

453
00:15:46,343 --> 00:15:48,182
Watch what the institution did next,

454
00:15:48,183 --> 00:15:49,741
because it is the tell.

455
00:15:49,801 --> 00:15:51,981
The prosecutions quietly stopped —

456
00:15:52,041 --> 00:15:55,021
from forty-two in a single year to two,

457
00:15:55,081 --> 00:15:56,241
then zero —

458
00:15:56,301 --> 00:15:57,701
with no announcement,

459
00:15:57,761 --> 00:16:00,181
no review of the convictions already won,

460
00:16:00,241 --> 00:16:02,580
no letter to the people already ruined.

461
00:16:02,640 --> 00:16:04,240
In March 2015,

462
00:16:04,300 --> 00:16:06,820
the Post Office sacked Second Sight and,

463
00:16:06,880 --> 00:16:09,800
as Arbuthnot told the House of Commons that week,

464
00:16:09,860 --> 00:16:12,261
told it to destroy all its papers.

465
00:16:12,321 --> 00:16:13,901
In January 2015,

466
00:16:13,961 --> 00:16:15,600
preparing to face a committee of MPs

467
00:16:15,601 --> 00:16:17,781
on the question of remote access,

468
00:16:17,841 --> 00:16:19,720
chief executive Paula Vennells wrote

469
00:16:19,721 --> 00:16:21,540
in an email disclosed years later

470
00:16:21,541 --> 00:16:23,341
to the public inquiry:

471
00:16:23,401 --> 00:16:26,261
"I need to say no it is not possible.

472
00:16:26,321 --> 00:16:28,280
A High Court judgment would later quote what

473
00:16:28,281 --> 00:16:30,600
the chief executive's office was asking behind

474
00:16:30,601 --> 00:16:32,521
closed doors that January:

475
00:16:32,581 --> 00:16:34,270
"What is the true answer?

476
00:16:34,330 --> 00:16:35,729
I hope it is that we know

477
00:16:35,730 --> 00:16:37,728
this is not possible.

478
00:16:37,788 --> 00:16:39,307
Its formal answer to Parliament

479
00:16:39,308 --> 00:16:41,748
that same winter was absolute:

480
00:16:41,808 --> 00:16:44,287
there is "no functionality in Horizon for either

481
00:16:44,288 --> 00:16:48,208
a branch, Post Office or Fujitsu to edit,

482
00:16:48,268 --> 00:16:51,107
manipulate or remove transaction data once it

483
00:16:51,108 --> 00:16:53,725
has been recorded in a branch's accounts.

484
00:16:53,785 --> 00:16:55,564
And when the BBC's Panorama put

485
00:16:55,565 --> 00:16:57,304
the question directly that summer,

486
00:16:57,305 --> 00:17:00,584
with a former Fujitsu engineer named Richard Roll

487
00:17:00,585 --> 00:17:01,884
saying on camera

488
00:17:01,885 --> 00:17:03,624
that engineers could and did reach

489
00:17:03,625 --> 00:17:05,105
into branch accounts,

490
00:17:05,165 --> 00:17:07,307
the Post Office denied it again.

491
00:17:07,367 --> 00:17:09,086
Every part of that sentence would die

492
00:17:09,087 --> 00:17:10,301
in a courtroom.

493
00:17:10,361 --> 00:17:12,300
It just needed someone with twenty years

494
00:17:12,301 --> 00:17:15,816
of patience to carry it there.

495
00:17:15,876 --> 00:17:18,484
The army had been forming since 2009.

496
00:17:18,544 --> 00:17:20,904
In May of that year, a trade magazine —

497
00:17:20,964 --> 00:17:22,144
Computer Weekly —

498
00:17:22,204 --> 00:17:24,003
had published the first story anyone dared

499
00:17:24,004 --> 00:17:24,824
to print,

500
00:17:24,884 --> 00:17:27,844
by a young reporter named Rebecca Thomson:

501
00:17:27,904 --> 00:17:30,044
seven sub-postmasters,

502
00:17:30,104 --> 00:17:33,320
seven impossible shortfalls, one system.

503
00:17:33,380 --> 00:17:35,849
Alan Bates was one of the seven.

504
00:17:35,909 --> 00:17:36,708
That November,

505
00:17:36,709 --> 00:17:38,568
he hired a village hall in Fenny Compton,

506
00:17:38,569 --> 00:17:39,509
Warwickshire —

507
00:17:39,569 --> 00:17:41,148
picked more or less at random,

508
00:17:41,149 --> 00:17:43,561
because it sat in the middle of England —

509
00:17:43,569 --> 00:17:45,994
and invited whoever else was out there.

510
00:17:46,054 --> 00:17:48,570
A couple of dozen strangers walked in.

511
00:17:48,630 --> 00:17:50,170
They had never met.

512
00:17:50,230 --> 00:17:52,569
Each of them had been told, for years,

513
00:17:52,570 --> 00:17:54,557
that they were the only one.

514
00:17:54,617 --> 00:17:56,696
They looked around a room full of only-ones,

515
00:17:56,697 --> 00:17:58,416
and they gave themselves a name:

516
00:17:58,417 --> 00:18:01,359
the Justice for Subpostmasters Alliance.

517
00:18:01,419 --> 00:18:03,118
The press lane stayed narrow,

518
00:18:03,119 --> 00:18:05,142
but it never quite closed.

519
00:18:05,202 --> 00:18:06,882
In November 2010

520
00:18:06,942 --> 00:18:09,101
a radio reporter named Nick Wallis took a call

521
00:18:09,102 --> 00:18:11,721
from a man whose pregnant wife had just been sent

522
00:18:11,722 --> 00:18:15,202
to prison over a till that would not balance —

523
00:18:15,262 --> 00:18:17,702
Seema Misra's husband, Davinder.

524
00:18:17,762 --> 00:18:19,801
Wallis spent the next decade on the story

525
00:18:19,802 --> 00:18:21,701
when almost nobody else would touch it,

526
00:18:21,702 --> 00:18:25,162
on regional television, then in Private Eye,

527
00:18:25,222 --> 00:18:26,640
eventually in a book.

528
00:18:26,700 --> 00:18:28,939
The facts were available for years to anyone

529
00:18:28,940 --> 00:18:30,051
who looked.

530
00:18:30,111 --> 00:18:31,744
Almost nobody looked.

531
00:18:31,804 --> 00:18:34,084
What followed was not a montage;

532
00:18:34,144 --> 00:18:36,023
it was a grind that consumed the better part

533
00:18:36,024 --> 00:18:37,431
of two decades.

534
00:18:37,491 --> 00:18:38,770
Bates ran the campaign

535
00:18:38,771 --> 00:18:40,430
the way he had run his branch —

536
00:18:40,431 --> 00:18:44,511
from a kitchen table, on a laptop, with files.

537
00:18:44,571 --> 00:18:47,378
He wrote to ministers and was patted on the head.

538
00:18:47,379 --> 00:18:49,098
He gathered members in the dozens,

539
00:18:49,099 --> 00:18:50,545
then the hundreds.

540
00:18:50,605 --> 00:18:52,465
And in 2017,

541
00:18:52,525 --> 00:18:56,864
five hundred and fifty-five sub-postmasters,

542
00:18:56,865 --> 00:19:00,384
bankrolled by litigation funders because none
of them had money left,

543
00:19:00,385 --> 00:19:02,364
sued the Post Office in the High Court

544
00:19:02,365 --> 00:19:03,489
under a single group action.

545
00:19:03,490 --> 00:19:05,110
The lead name on the claim,

546
00:19:05,170 --> 00:19:07,610
alphabetically and in every other sense:

547
00:19:07,670 --> 00:19:09,628
Bates versus Post Office.

548
00:19:09,688 --> 00:19:12,482
The institution fought like an institution.

549
00:19:12,542 --> 00:19:15,568
It ran up costs designed to outlast the funders.

550
00:19:15,628 --> 00:19:17,008
In March 2019,

551
00:19:17,068 --> 00:19:19,527
when the first judgment savaged its sub-postmaster

552
00:19:19,528 --> 00:19:23,088
contracts, it did something almost unheard of:

553
00:19:23,148 --> 00:19:25,948
mid-trial, it applied to remove the judge,

554
00:19:26,008 --> 00:19:28,267
Mr Justice Fraser, from the case.

555
00:19:28,327 --> 00:19:30,026
The application failed within weeks,

556
00:19:30,027 --> 00:19:32,514
and the appeal court refused to touch it.

557
00:19:32,574 --> 00:19:34,574
And in December 2019,

558
00:19:34,634 --> 00:19:36,073
Fraser handed down the judgment

559
00:19:36,074 --> 00:19:38,273
the Post Office had spent twenty years making sure

560
00:19:38,274 --> 00:19:39,826
no one would ever write.

561
00:19:39,886 --> 00:19:41,305
The shoeboxes of receipts

562
00:19:41,306 --> 00:19:43,805
and the backed-up backups had finally met a judge

563
00:19:43,806 --> 00:19:45,281
who read every page.

564
00:19:45,341 --> 00:19:46,821
Legacy Horizon, he found,

565
00:19:46,881 --> 00:19:48,832
was "not remotely robust.

566
00:19:48,892 --> 00:19:50,071
The Post Office's approach

567
00:19:50,072 --> 00:19:52,152
to the mounting evidence, he wrote,

568
00:19:52,212 --> 00:19:54,931
"demonstrates a simple institutional obstinacy

569
00:19:54,932 --> 00:19:58,231
or refusal to consider any possible alternatives

570
00:19:58,232 --> 00:20:00,532
to their view of Horizon" —

571
00:20:00,592 --> 00:20:03,351
its insistence on the system's integrity amounted

572
00:20:03,352 --> 00:20:06,711
to "the 21st century equivalent of maintaining

573
00:20:06,712 --> 00:20:08,405
that the earth is flat.

574
00:20:08,465 --> 00:20:09,264
And the denials

575
00:20:09,265 --> 00:20:11,584
about Bracknell collapsed entirely.

576
00:20:11,644 --> 00:20:14,283
Remote access to branch accounts, the court found,

577
00:20:14,284 --> 00:20:15,604
"does exist;

578
00:20:15,664 --> 00:20:17,403
such remote access is possible

579
00:20:17,404 --> 00:20:19,604
by employees within Fujitsu;

580
00:20:19,664 --> 00:20:22,124
it does exist specifically by design;

581
00:20:22,184 --> 00:20:24,237
and it has been used in the past.

582
00:20:24,297 --> 00:20:27,238
The Post Office's years of public assurances were,

583
00:20:27,277 --> 00:20:30,946
in the judge's words, "specifically wrong in fact.

584
00:20:31,006 --> 00:20:31,985
Before Christmas,

585
00:20:31,986 --> 00:20:33,965
Fraser referred his concerns about the evidence

586
00:20:33,966 --> 00:20:36,125
of Fujitsu's witnesses to the Director

587
00:20:36,126 --> 00:20:37,980
of Public Prosecutions.

588
00:20:38,040 --> 00:20:40,370
The 555 had won.

589
00:20:40,430 --> 00:20:42,540
Now look at what winning bought them.

590
00:20:42,541 --> 00:20:45,521
The Post Office had in fact already seen enough:

591
00:20:45,581 --> 00:20:48,741
days before the judgment came down, it settled —

592
00:20:48,801 --> 00:20:52,023
fifty-seven point seven five million pounds.

593
00:20:52,083 --> 00:20:54,302
And after the litigation funders and the lawyers

594
00:20:54,303 --> 00:20:57,102
who had made the case possible took their share,

595
00:20:57,103 --> 00:20:58,642
the sub-postmasters were left

596
00:20:58,643 --> 00:21:01,193
with roughly twelve million between them.

597
00:21:01,253 --> 00:21:02,692
About twenty thousand pounds each,

598
00:21:02,693 --> 00:21:05,998
for ruined businesses, bankruptcies, and prison.

599
00:21:06,058 --> 00:21:07,737
Alan Bates had beaten the Post Office

600
00:21:07,738 --> 00:21:08,457
in open court

601
00:21:08,458 --> 00:21:11,406
and could not even cover his members' losses.

602
00:21:11,466 --> 00:21:14,407
But the judgment was never really about the money.

603
00:21:14,413 --> 00:21:15,721
It was a key.

604
00:21:15,781 --> 00:21:17,180
Criminal convictions built

605
00:21:17,181 --> 00:21:19,240
on a machine now judicially certified

606
00:21:19,241 --> 00:21:22,421
as unreliable could finally be appealed —

607
00:21:22,481 --> 00:21:25,020
and the Criminal Cases Review Commission began

608
00:21:25,021 --> 00:21:26,841
referring them in batches.

609
00:21:26,901 --> 00:21:30,060
In December 2020, the first six convictions fell

610
00:21:30,061 --> 00:21:31,739
at Southwark Crown Court.

611
00:21:31,799 --> 00:21:33,818
And on the twenty-third of April 2021,

612
00:21:33,819 --> 00:21:35,578
at the Royal Courts of Justice,

613
00:21:35,579 --> 00:21:38,238
the Court of Appeal ruled on thirty-nine more —

614
00:21:38,239 --> 00:21:40,909
and did not merely quash them.

615
00:21:40,969 --> 00:21:43,206
It found the Post Office had known.

616
00:21:43,266 --> 00:21:46,850
"POL knew that there were problems with Horizon.

617
00:21:46,910 --> 00:21:48,549
POL knew that SPMs around

618
00:21:48,550 --> 00:21:49,749
the country had complained

619
00:21:49,750 --> 00:21:52,990
of inexplicable discrepancies… In short,

620
00:21:53,050 --> 00:21:54,769
POL knew that there were serious issues

621
00:21:54,770 --> 00:21:57,181
about the reliability of Horizon.

622
00:21:57,241 --> 00:21:58,781
It had, the court said,

623
00:21:58,841 --> 00:22:01,740
"effectively steamrolled over any SPM who sought

624
00:22:01,741 --> 00:22:03,481
to challenge its accuracy" —

625
00:22:03,541 --> 00:22:06,400
and its failures were "so egregious as to make

626
00:22:06,401 --> 00:22:08,980
the prosecution of any of the 'Horizon cases'

627
00:22:08,981 --> 00:22:11,924
an affront to the conscience of the court.

628
00:22:11,984 --> 00:22:14,455
On the pavement outside stood Jo Hamilton,

629
00:22:14,464 --> 00:22:16,683
eighteen years after a helpline talked her debt

630
00:22:16,684 --> 00:22:19,004
from two thousand pounds to four —

631
00:22:19,064 --> 00:22:21,930
no longer, in the eyes of the law, a thief.

632
00:22:21,990 --> 00:22:24,790
Cameras caught her half-laughing, half-crying,

633
00:22:24,850 --> 00:22:26,410
holding the paperwork.

634
00:22:26,470 --> 00:22:28,196
Thirty-nine names cleared.

635
00:22:28,256 --> 00:22:32,396
Then more, in batches through 2022 and 2023 —

636
00:22:32,456 --> 00:22:34,069
about a hundred in all.

637
00:22:34,129 --> 00:22:37,549
Which left roughly nine hundred convictions still

638
00:22:37,609 --> 00:22:41,188
standing, held by people now old, or ill, or dead,

639
00:22:41,189 --> 00:22:43,969
many of whom would not go near a courtroom again

640
00:22:43,970 --> 00:22:44,552
for anything.

641
00:22:44,553 --> 00:22:46,693
At the pace the system was managing,

642
00:22:46,753 --> 00:22:48,973
the last of them would die convicted.

643
00:22:49,033 --> 00:22:52,384
That is where this story stood on New Year's Eve,
2023.

644
00:22:52,444 --> 00:22:54,523
What broke the logjam was not a judgment,

645
00:22:54,524 --> 00:22:56,213
and not a report.

646
00:22:56,273 --> 00:22:59,731
It was a television schedule.

647
00:22:59,791 --> 00:23:02,451
On the first of January 2024,

648
00:23:02,511 --> 00:23:05,611
ITV began a four-night drama called Mr Bates vs

649
00:23:05,671 --> 00:23:06,991
the Post Office —

650
00:23:07,051 --> 00:23:10,011
actors playing Bates, Hamilton, Misra,

651
00:23:10,071 --> 00:23:12,751
the helpline calls, the shredded minutes,

652
00:23:12,811 --> 00:23:14,474
the whole quarter-century.

653
00:23:14,534 --> 00:23:16,260
Nothing in it was new.

654
00:23:16,320 --> 00:23:18,099
Every fact had been in the public record

655
00:23:18,100 --> 00:23:21,399
for years, in judgments almost nobody outside

656
00:23:21,400 --> 00:23:23,154
the campaign had read.

657
00:23:23,214 --> 00:23:25,213
But close to ten million people watched it

658
00:23:25,214 --> 00:23:26,434
in a week —

659
00:23:26,494 --> 00:23:28,974
thirteen and a half million by spring —

660
00:23:29,034 --> 00:23:31,133
and Britain went from not knowing the story

661
00:23:31,134 --> 00:23:34,274
to being unable to think about anything else.

662
00:23:34,334 --> 00:23:37,193
Within days, more than 1.2 million people signed

663
00:23:37,194 --> 00:23:41,094
a petition demanding Paula Vennells lose her CBE —

664
00:23:41,154 --> 00:23:43,173
the honour she had been given in 2019,

665
00:23:43,174 --> 00:23:45,673
for services to the Post Office.

666
00:23:45,733 --> 00:23:47,672
On the ninth of January she announced:

667
00:23:47,673 --> 00:23:50,571
"I return my CBE with immediate effect.

668
00:23:50,631 --> 00:23:51,370
On the tenth,

669
00:23:51,371 --> 00:23:53,070
the Prime Minister stood up in Parliament

670
00:23:53,071 --> 00:23:55,701
and promised something without precedent.

671
00:23:55,761 --> 00:23:56,961
The running number —

672
00:23:57,021 --> 00:23:59,480
employees of the Post Office or Fujitsu convicted

673
00:23:59,481 --> 00:24:00,841
for any of this —

674
00:24:00,901 --> 00:24:03,524
stood at zero on the night the drama aired.

675
00:24:03,584 --> 00:24:05,683
It is worth watching closely through the reckoning

676
00:24:05,684 --> 00:24:07,028
that follows.

677
00:24:07,088 --> 00:24:09,308
On the twenty-fourth of May 2024,

678
00:24:09,368 --> 00:24:11,947
the Post Office (Horizon System) Offences Act

679
00:24:11,948 --> 00:24:13,551
received royal assent.

680
00:24:13,611 --> 00:24:15,850
Its first sentence does something no British law

681
00:24:15,851 --> 00:24:17,951
had ever done at this scale:

682
00:24:18,011 --> 00:24:18,710
"Every conviction

683
00:24:18,711 --> 00:24:21,850
to which this Act applies is quashed on the coming

684
00:24:21,851 --> 00:24:23,650
into force of this Act.

685
00:24:23,710 --> 00:24:25,018
Not reviewed.

686
00:24:25,078 --> 00:24:26,433
Not referred.

687
00:24:26,493 --> 00:24:27,713
Quashed —

688
00:24:27,773 --> 00:24:28,772
hundreds of convictions,

689
00:24:28,773 --> 00:24:30,993
at the stroke of the King's pen,

690
00:24:31,053 --> 00:24:33,272
most of them without any court ever looking

691
00:24:33,273 --> 00:24:35,557
at the individual case again.

692
00:24:35,617 --> 00:24:38,776
In England and Wales alone, six hundred

693
00:24:38,777 --> 00:24:41,056
and nineteen people have so far been identified

694
00:24:41,057 --> 00:24:42,139
as cleared by the Act.

695
00:24:42,140 --> 00:24:44,493
Parliament had already, back in January,

696
00:24:44,540 --> 00:24:47,219
rushed through a first Act to get money moving;

697
00:24:47,220 --> 00:24:49,359
the cleared were offered six hundred thousand

698
00:24:49,360 --> 00:24:51,879
pounds each, full and final —

699
00:24:51,880 --> 00:24:53,239
or a longer, harder road

700
00:24:53,240 --> 00:24:55,489
if they believed their losses were bigger.

701
00:24:55,490 --> 00:24:58,149
The country's most senior former judges rose

702
00:24:58,150 --> 00:24:59,889
in the House of Lords to object,

703
00:24:59,890 --> 00:25:03,270
and their objection measures the failure:

704
00:25:03,330 --> 00:25:05,610
Parliament does not quash convictions,

705
00:25:05,670 --> 00:25:07,910
said the former Lord Chief Justice —

706
00:25:07,970 --> 00:25:10,033
that is a matter for the courts.

707
00:25:10,093 --> 00:25:11,355
He was right.

708
00:25:11,415 --> 00:25:14,035
That is exactly how bad it had become:

709
00:25:14,095 --> 00:25:15,734
the miscarriage was too big for the courts

710
00:25:15,735 --> 00:25:16,617
that caused it,

711
00:25:16,675 --> 00:25:18,974
and the constitution had to be bent to clear

712
00:25:18,975 --> 00:25:20,711
the innocent in bulk.

713
00:25:20,771 --> 00:25:23,844
The calendar of that week is its own verdict.

714
00:25:23,904 --> 00:25:25,583
On the very Friday the Act received

715
00:25:25,584 --> 00:25:28,504
the King's assent, across London,

716
00:25:28,564 --> 00:25:30,683
Paula Vennells was completing three days

717
00:25:30,684 --> 00:25:33,004
of evidence to the public inquiry —

718
00:25:33,064 --> 00:25:35,543
under a barrister's questions about the email

719
00:25:35,544 --> 00:25:37,704
that said I need to say no,

720
00:25:37,764 --> 00:25:40,537
about what she had been told, and when.

721
00:25:40,597 --> 00:25:42,277
She wept repeatedly.

722
00:25:42,337 --> 00:25:45,038
"I was too trusting," she told the inquiry.

723
00:25:45,098 --> 00:25:46,338
Three months earlier,

724
00:25:46,398 --> 00:25:48,838
the King had formally erased her honour;

725
00:25:48,898 --> 00:25:50,897
the notice in the official Gazette records

726
00:25:50,898 --> 00:25:53,929
that her CBE was "cancelled and annulled.

727
00:25:53,989 --> 00:25:55,108
It remains, to date, one

728
00:25:55,109 --> 00:25:57,508
of the very few individual consequences anyone

729
00:25:57,509 --> 00:25:59,848
connected to this scandal has faced.

730
00:25:59,908 --> 00:26:02,928
In July 2025, the inquiry's chairman,

731
00:26:02,988 --> 00:26:04,268
Sir Wyn Williams,

732
00:26:04,328 --> 00:26:07,152
published the first volume of his final report —

733
00:26:07,188 --> 00:26:08,739
the human accounting.

734
00:26:08,799 --> 00:26:10,918
Roughly one thousand people prosecuted

735
00:26:10,919 --> 00:26:13,404
and convicted on Horizon evidence.

736
00:26:13,464 --> 00:26:15,523
About ten thousand people now eligible

737
00:26:15,524 --> 00:26:16,862
for redress.

738
00:26:16,922 --> 00:26:19,681
At least thirteen suicides linked to the scandal,

739
00:26:19,682 --> 00:26:21,667
a number he said may be higher.

740
00:26:21,727 --> 00:26:24,139
His word for the impact was "disastrous,"

741
00:26:24,167 --> 00:26:26,386
and his finding was that these people were victims

742
00:26:26,387 --> 00:26:28,987
of "wholly unacceptable behaviour" —

743
00:26:29,047 --> 00:26:31,986
by individuals, and by the Post Office and Fujitsu

744
00:26:31,987 --> 00:26:34,181
"as institutions.

745
00:26:34,241 --> 00:26:35,980
He attached nineteen recommendations,

746
00:26:35,981 --> 00:26:39,125
and a deadline for the institutions to answer him.

747
00:26:39,185 --> 00:26:40,865
By the summer of 2026,

748
00:26:40,925 --> 00:26:44,424
the government's ledger showed
about one point six billion pounds paid

749
00:26:44,425 --> 00:26:45,064
out to more

750
00:26:45,065 --> 00:26:48,624
than twelve thousand nine hundred claimants —

751
00:26:48,625 --> 00:26:52,785
real money at last, though Sir Alan Bates,

752
00:26:52,845 --> 00:26:54,464
knighted in 2024, was by then writing

753
00:26:54,465 --> 00:26:56,444
that the schemes had been turned

754
00:26:56,445 --> 00:26:57,964
into "quasi-kangaroo courts" —

755
00:26:57,965 --> 00:26:59,384
his own offer less than half

756
00:26:59,385 --> 00:27:00,602
of what he had lost.

757
00:27:00,662 --> 00:27:01,861
Fujitsu told Parliament

758
00:27:01,862 --> 00:27:05,061
in January 2024 it had a "moral obligation"

759
00:27:05,062 --> 00:27:06,289
to contribute.

760
00:27:06,349 --> 00:27:08,289
As of the middle of 2026,

761
00:27:08,349 --> 00:27:10,069
it had contributed nothing —

762
00:27:10,129 --> 00:27:12,368
and its engineers still run Horizon,

763
00:27:12,369 --> 00:27:15,529
on a contract extended to 2027,

764
00:27:15,589 --> 00:27:17,268
because the machine at the centre of all

765
00:27:17,269 --> 00:27:19,749
of this is still, today,

766
00:27:19,809 --> 00:27:23,786
on the counters of Britain's post offices.

767
00:27:23,846 --> 00:27:25,386
So finish the count.

768
00:27:25,446 --> 00:27:27,665
Roughly one thousand people convicted on the word

769
00:27:27,666 --> 00:27:29,308
of a broken machine.

770
00:27:29,368 --> 00:27:31,507
About seven hundred of them prosecuted

771
00:27:31,508 --> 00:27:33,147
by their own employer —

772
00:27:33,148 --> 00:27:35,227
one a week, for fifteen years.

773
00:27:35,287 --> 00:27:36,567
By the BBC's count,

774
00:27:36,627 --> 00:27:39,208
two hundred and thirty-six of them went to prison.

775
00:27:39,209 --> 00:27:40,988
At least thirteen people are dead

776
00:27:40,989 --> 00:27:42,514
by their own hand.

777
00:27:42,574 --> 00:27:44,233
And the number of human beings convicted

778
00:27:44,234 --> 00:27:46,534
of any crime for doing this to them —

779
00:27:46,594 --> 00:27:49,614
after the shredded minutes, the buried advice,

780
00:27:49,674 --> 00:27:50,173
the denials

781
00:27:50,174 --> 00:27:52,753
a High Court judge called specifically wrong

782
00:27:52,754 --> 00:27:53,854
in fact —

783
00:27:53,914 --> 00:27:55,585
is still zero.

784
00:27:55,645 --> 00:27:57,304
A police investigation with more

785
00:27:57,305 --> 00:28:00,064
than a hundred officers has fifty-three people

786
00:28:00,065 --> 00:28:01,684
under examination;

787
00:28:01,685 --> 00:28:04,604
it does not expect to put files before prosecutors

788
00:28:04,605 --> 00:28:06,566
until late 2027.

789
00:28:06,626 --> 00:28:08,125
The inquiry volumes that will name

790
00:28:08,126 --> 00:28:10,726
the culpable are, as of this summer,

791
00:28:10,786 --> 00:28:12,114
still unpublished.

792
00:28:12,174 --> 00:28:13,073
The detective leading

793
00:28:13,074 --> 00:28:15,874
the criminal case said it plainly this spring:

794
00:28:15,934 --> 00:28:19,754
many victims are old now, some are already gone —

795
00:28:19,814 --> 00:28:22,166
"we do not have the luxury of time."

796
00:28:22,226 --> 00:28:24,125
Somewhere in England tomorrow morning,

797
00:28:24,126 --> 00:28:26,815
a shutter will go up over a post-office counter,

798
00:28:26,816 --> 00:28:30,082
and the screen behind it will still say Horizon.

799
00:28:30,142 --> 00:28:31,961
The system was told the truth about itself

800
00:28:31,962 --> 00:28:33,801
in 2010, in writing,

801
00:28:33,802 --> 00:28:37,116
and calculated that the truth was unaffordable.

802
00:28:37,176 --> 00:28:38,975
It spent the next decade buying time

803
00:28:38,976 --> 00:28:40,155
with other people's lives —

804
00:28:40,156 --> 00:28:41,815
and the bill, so far,

805
00:28:41,816 --> 00:28:44,235
has been paid by everyone except the people

806
00:28:44,236 --> 00:28:45,357
who wrote it.

807
00:28:45,417 --> 00:28:48,211
One more line of the ledger deserves its place.

808
00:28:48,271 --> 00:28:50,918
For years, a helpline told frightened people,

809
00:28:50,951 --> 00:28:52,651
one call at a time,

810
00:28:52,711 --> 00:28:54,409
that they were the only one.

811
00:28:54,469 --> 00:28:57,077
There turned out to be ten thousand of them.

812
00:28:57,137 --> 00:28:58,216
The sentence was a lie

813
00:28:58,217 --> 00:28:59,776
the first time it was spoken

814
00:28:59,777 --> 00:29:01,577
and a lie every time after —

815
00:29:01,637 --> 00:29:03,677
and the cure for it, in the end,

816
00:29:03,737 --> 00:29:06,015
was not a court, and not a law.

817
00:29:06,075 --> 00:29:07,914
It was a village hall in the middle of England,

818
00:29:07,915 --> 00:29:10,894
and a handful of ruined shopkeepers discovering

819
00:29:10,895 --> 00:29:13,235
that the only-ones were an army.

820
00:29:13,295 --> 00:29:13,834
If you think

821
00:29:13,835 --> 00:29:15,374
a machine's word should never outweigh

822
00:29:15,375 --> 00:29:16,654
a human being's —

823
00:29:16,655 --> 00:29:18,114
and that someone, eventually,

824
00:29:18,115 --> 00:29:20,575
must answer for the ones it buried —

825
00:29:20,635 --> 00:29:23,714
hit like, so the next story like this gets told

826
00:29:23,715 --> 00:29:26,021
before it takes twenty years.
